sec.p:
For each baseline period, we calculated the mean interbeat interval, the standard deviation of interbeat intervals, and RMSSD. RMSSD was calculated as


RMSSD=1N−1∑i=1N−1(IBIi+1−IBIi)2
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathrm{RMSSD}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{N-1} \sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\left(I B I_{i+1}-I B I_{i}\right)^{2}}$$\end{document}



where IBIiand IBIi+1are consecutive interbeat intervals. RMSSD therefore increases when adjacent cardiac intervals differ more strongly in duration. To compare animals with different mean cardiac intervals, RMSSD was normalized to the mean interbeat interval:


normalised RMSSD =100× RMSSD  mean IBI 
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \text {normalised RMSSD }=100 \times \frac{\text { RMSSD }}{\text { mean IBI }}$$\end{document}



We additionally calculated the coefficient of variation of interbeat intervals, CVNN:


CVNN=100× SD of IBI  mean IBI 
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathrm{CVNN}=100 \times \frac{\text { SD of IBI }}{\text { mean IBI }}$$\end{document}



RMSSD and CVNN provide complementary descriptions of cardiac variability. RMSSD emphasizes changes between consecutive cardiac intervals, whereas CVNN measures the overall dispersion of all intervals within the analyzed period. Metrics were calculated separately for each baseline period and then averaged within each fish for visualization and fish-level descriptive statistics.

To determine whether normalized RMSSD increased gradually across development or underwent a more discrete developmental transition, we compared four linear mixed-effects models. The models described age as: (1) a continuous linear predictor; (2) a binary transition between 4 and 5 dpf; (3) a transition between 4 and 5 dpf followed by a continuous slope from 5 to 12 dpf; or (4) a categorical developmental-stage variable. All models were fitted to log-transformed normalized RMSSD values from individual baseline periods and included trial number as a fixed effect and fish identity as a random intercept:
log⁡(normalized RMSSD)∼developmental terms+trial+(1|fish). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \log (\text{normalized RMSSD}) \sim \text{developmental terms}+ \text{trial}+ (1 \mid \text{fish}).$$\end{document}
Models were initially fitted by maximum likelihood and compared using the Akaike information criterion, Bayesian information criterion, differences in Akaike information criterion, and Akaike weights. The selected model was refitted using restricted maximum likelihood for coefficient estimation and confidence intervals. Population-level predictions were evaluated at the mean trial number and back-transformed to the original normalized-RMSSD scale. The developmental data were best described by a transition at 5 dpf followed by a more gradual subsequent increase.

Because heart-rate variability can depend on average heart rate, we next modeled absolute RMSSD in milliseconds as a function of mean interbeat interval and developmental stage. Absolute RMSSD and mean interbeat interval were log transformed. We compared models containing: (1) mean interbeat interval alone; (2) mean interbeat interval and the piecewise developmental transition; (3) mean interbeat interval and categorical developmental stage; or (4) an interaction between categorical developmental stage and mean interbeat interval. All models included trial number as a fixed effect and fish identity as a random intercept:
log⁡(RMSSD)∼age×log⁡(mean IBI)+trial+(1|fish). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \log (\text{RMSSD}) \sim \text{age} \times \log (\text{mean IBI}) + \text{trial} + (1 \mid \text{fish}).$$\end{document}
The interaction model received the strongest support by Akaike information criterion, indicating that the relationship between average interbeat interval and cardiac variability differed across developmental stages. Age-specific fitted relationships and population-level 95% confidence intervals were generated from this model with trial number held at its centered value.

Let HR(t) denote the heart-rate time series (Hz) sampled at interval Δt; let ri(t) be the calcium signal (ΔF/F₀) for neuron i. Signals were detrended (second-order polynomial) and z-scored within fish/session.
(1)
HRz(t)=HR(t)−μHRσHR \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} H R_{z}(t)=\frac{H R(t)-\mu_{H R}}{\sigma_{H R}} \tag{1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Neural activity was modeled as a convolution of HR with a causal kernel ki(τ), plus a bias bi.
(4)
r^i(t)=bi+∑τ=0Lki(τ)HRz(t−τ) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \hat{r}_{i}(t)=b_{i}+\sum_{\tau=0}^{L} k_{i}(\tau) H R_{z}(t-\tau) \tag{4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
We applied a static nonlinearity for saturation effects:
(5)
r^i(t)=11+e−(bi+∑τ=0Lki(τ)HRz(t−τ)). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \hat{r}_{i}(t)=\frac{1}{1+e^{-\left(b_{i}+\sum_{\tau=0}^{L} k_{i}(\tau) H R_{z}(t-\tau)\right)}} . \tag{5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Kernels were estimated by ridge regression:
(6)
minki,bi‖yi−(1bi+Xki)‖22+λ‖Dki‖22, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \min _{k_{i}, b_{i}}\left\|y_{i}-\left(\mathbf{1} b_{i}+X k_{i}\right)\right\|_{2}^{2}+\lambda\left\|D k_{i}\right\|_{2}^{2}, \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
With D=I unless otherwise stated, λ was chosen by inner cross-validation.

Blocked 5-fold cross-validation: 80% train, 20% test per fold. Model performance was quantified as cross- validated R2:
(8)
RCV2=1−∑t∈Ttest(y(t)−y^(t))2∑t∈Ttest(y(t)−y¯train)2. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} R_{\mathrm{CV}}^{2}=1-\frac{\sum_{t \in \mathcal{T}_{\text {test }}}(y(t)-\hat{y}(t))^{2}}{\sum_{t \in \mathcal{T}_{\text {test }}}\left(y(t)-\bar{y}_{\text {train }}\right)^{2}} . \tag{8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Kernels were L²-normalized for visualization in heatmaps:
((9)
k~i=ki‖ki‖2. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \tilde{k}_{i}=\frac{k_{i}}{\left\|k_{i}\right\|_{2}} . \tag{(9} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Normalized kernels were projected into kernel principal component analysis (PCA) space. Phases were defined from the first two PCs:
(10)
ϕi=atan2(v2ki,v1ki),ϕi∈(−π,π]. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \phi_{i}=\operatorname{atan} 2\left(v_{2} k_{i}, v_{1} k_{i}\right), \quad \phi_{i} \in(-\pi, \pi] . \tag{10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
